### Frontend Design System

This file documents the **actual design conventions in the codebase**, not
aspirations. The two structural rules ("no inline CSS/JS in templates",
"shared assets under `front/static/`") live in
`05-code-style-and-structure.mdc § Frontend (HTML/CSS/JS)`. This file
covers visual language, components, naming and asset wiring.

### Design Token Systems (two coexisting scopes)

There are **two namespaced token systems**, by design — do not mix them.

| Scope | File | Prefix | Theme |
|-------|------|--------|-------|
| App shell (navbar, pages, modals, forms, KPI tiles, etc.) | `src/front/static/global/css/main.css` | `--db-*` | Clarity indigo accent on warm neutrals |
| OntoViz canvas (entity boxes, relationships, toolbar) | `src/front/static/global/ontoviz/css/ontoviz-variables.css` | `--ovz-*` | Dedicated OntoViz tokens (kept separate) |

**Rules:**

- Always use a token. Never hard-code a colour, shadow, radius or transition
  that has a token equivalent.
- Inside `static/global/ontoviz/**`, use `--ovz-*` tokens only.
- Everywhere else under `static/**` and `templates/**`, use `--db-*` tokens.
- Do not introduce a third namespace. If a new token is needed, extend the
  appropriate file (`main.css :root` or `ontoviz-variables.css :root`).

### Colour Palette (`--db-*`, CSS static defaults)

The primary family below (`--db-primary` and derived tokens) are **static
fallback defaults** declared in `main.css :root`. At runtime, the server injects
a `<style id="uiBrandingTokens">` block that overrides the entire primary family
from the stored brand configuration (see *Runtime Theme Contract* below). All
other tokens are **fixed** — they are not overridden at runtime.

| Role | Token | CSS static default |
|------|-------|--------------------|
| Brand primary *(configurable)* | `--db-primary` | `#737373` |
| Primary dark / darker *(configurable)* | `--db-primary-dark`, `--db-primary-darker` | `#626262`, `#515151` |
| Primary tint *(configurable)* | `--db-primary-light` | `rgba(115, 115, 115, 0.1)` |
| Primary soft fill *(derived from RGB)* | `--db-primary-soft`, `--db-primary-soft-hover` | `rgba(var(--db-primary-rgb), 0.18)` / `0.28` |
| Primary RGB components *(configurable)* | `--db-primary-rgb` | `115, 115, 115` |
| Hover well *(configurable)* | `--db-hover-indigo` | `rgba(115, 115, 115, 0.06)` |
| Focus ring *(configurable)* | `--db-focus-ring` | `0 0 0 0.2rem rgba(115, 115, 115, 0.18)` |
| Shadow primary *(configurable)* | `--db-shadow-primary` | `0 8px 20px rgba(115, 115, 115, 0.35)` |
| On-primary foreground *(configurable)* | `--db-on-primary` | `#FFFFFF` |
| Selected text *(configurable)* | `--db-primary-selected-text` | derived dark variant |
| Warm canvas / surface / line *(fixed)* | `--db-canvas-warm`, `--db-surface-warm`, `--db-line-soft` | `#FAFAF9`, `#FFFFFF`, `#F4F2EF` |
| App chrome/control surfaces *(fixed)* | `--db-navbar-bg`, `--db-surface-warm` | `#FFFFFF`, `#FFFFFF` |
| Borders *(fixed)* | `--db-border`, `--db-border-light` | `#ECEAE6`, `#F4F2EF` |
| Text *(fixed)* | `--db-text`, `--db-text-secondary`, `--db-text-muted` | `#191817`, `#6B6864`, `#6F6B65` |
| Semantic tinted status *(fixed)* | `--db-status-success/warning/danger/info/draft` + `*-soft-bg` | success / warning / danger / info / draft on soft backgrounds |
| Shared radii *(fixed)* | `--db-radius-control`, `--db-radius-card`, `--db-radius-chip` | `2px`, `2px`, `2px` |
| Shadows *(fixed)* | `--db-shadow-sm`, `--db-shadow-md`, `--db-shadow-lg` | floating surfaces only |
| Transitions *(fixed)* | `--db-transition`, `--db-transition-fast` | `0.2s` / `0.15s ease` |

`--db-text-muted: #6F6B65` is the current accessible muted token and must remain
the lower-bound support/body-muted color.

### Runtime Theme Contract

Administrators can configure the application brand from **Settings →
Configuration → UI**. The runtime theme system follows these rules:

**Configurable primary family (one user input, many derived tokens)**

- The user supplies one `#RRGGBB` hex. The backend derives the full primary
  family: dark, darker, light/alpha variants, RGB components, hover well, focus
  ring, shadow, and a WCAG-contrast–safe `--db-on-primary` foreground (white or
  near-black, ≥ 4.5:1 ratio).
- `#737373` is the factory default; it is **not** an immutable runtime color.
  Any valid hex is accepted after admin save. The factory Aurora accent is
  `#6B6B6B`.
- The server injects these values as a `<style id="uiBrandingTokens">` block
  placed **after** the static CSS loads and **before** any `{% block extra_css
  %}` — this ensures no flash of unstyled primary color on first paint.

**Fixed semantic colors**

All `--db-status-*`, `--db-*-bg`, `--db-text-*`, `--db-border-*`,
`--db-navbar-bg`, `--db-subnav-bg`, radii, shadows, and transitions are **not
overridable** via the branding feature. They remain as defined in `main.css :root`.
`--db-subnav-bg` is retained as a compatibility token but is not the L2 row
surface: `#obSubnav` is transparent; workspace navigation uses the app switcher.
`--ovz-*` tokens are fully isolated and are never touched by runtime branding.

**First-paint rules**

- The middleware resolves cached branding only for HTML navigation requests
  (`Accept: text/html`). JSON/API requests are unaffected.
- On any resolution failure the middleware logs once and injects the default
  palette — there is no fallback flash to unstyled state.
- Do not set `--db-primary` or derived tokens via inline `style=""` from page
  code. The `#uiBrandingTokens` block is the single source for runtime overrides.

**Brand icon and title hooks**

- The brand title (`app_title`) is rendered server-side into the navbar, window
  `<title>`, and all `alt=` attributes that reference the app name. The Jinja
  helper `brand_page_title(page_title, request)` composes `"<page_title> –
  <app_title>"`.
- The brand logo URL is resolved from the stored data URL (custom logo) or the
  default `/static/global/img/favicon.svg`. It is injected into `<link
  rel="icon">`, the navbar `#brandLogoImg`, the Help modal, KG Chat, and the
  Ontology Map — all from the same `branding.logo_url` Jinja variable.
- From JavaScript, the Settings UI can preview changes locally by applying
  `document.documentElement.style.setProperty('--db-primary', hex)` and updating
  `textContent` / `src` on the relevant DOM elements. These inline overrides are
  removed on Discard or replaced on Save. Server-rendered state (from the next
  page load) is always authoritative.
- A dedicated **Reset icon** button (`#uiBrandingResetIconBtn`) in the Branding
  card reverts only the icon draft to the bundled default logo, leaving
  title/color drafts intact. It marks the draft dirty only when the saved logo
  differs from the default; the **Default** button (`#uiBrandingResetDefaultsBtn`)
  calls `resetUIBrandingDefaults()` to preview factory title/color/logo and
  immediately restore the factory default entity icon.
- Title and color inputs expose `aria-describedby` for help and error regions,
  and toggle `aria-invalid="true"` dynamically on validation failure. Swatches
  carry `role="img"` with stable `aria-label`/`title` values that update during
  preview to reflect the current resolved color. The component preview block is
  `aria-hidden="true"` (decorative).

**Aurora accent and cinematic gradient tokens (Aurora Pro)**

- The **Aurora** accent hex is a second admin-configurable input, alongside
  Primary, exposed in Settings → Configuration → UI → Theme (`aurora_color`,
  stored in the `ui_branding` global-config document). When omitted it is
  deterministically derived from Primary via an HSL hue rotation (see
  `back.core.helpers.UIBranding._derive_aurora_hex`) — there is no unstyled or
  undefined state.
- `--db-gradient-primary` (`linear-gradient(120deg, var(--db-primary),
  var(--db-gradient-end))`) is a defined design token but no component uses it
  any more: filled CTAs use the solid Create tokens (see below). `--db-gradient-end`
  is Primary mixed 45% toward Aurora, contrast-corrected server-side against
  `--db-on-primary` — never hardcode a second color on a button.
- `--db-gradient-rail` (`linear-gradient(180deg, var(--db-primary),
  var(--db-aurora))`) is reserved for the sidebar's 3px active-row leading bar
  only.
- `--db-halo` and `--db-canvas-warm` are the only Aurora-aware page-level
  treatments; forms, tables, and modals stay flat `--db-surface-warm` with no
  gradients.
- Resting cards (`.card`) stay flat: 1px border only, `box-shadow: none`.
  Elevation (`--db-shadow-*`) is reserved for floating surfaces
  (dropdowns, modals, overlays, transient toasts).

**Databricks button styles: Create (filled) and Share (outlined)**

- Settings → Configuration → UI → Theme exposes two colour inputs, next to
  Primary and Aurora: **Create button color** (`button_primary_color`) and
  **Share button color** (`button_secondary_color`). Both default to the
  Databricks Catalog browse page values (Create `#2272B4`, Share label
  `#11171C`) and are stored in the `ui_branding` global-config document.
- Blank or invalid stored values fall back to the defaults (never a load
  error), like Aurora. Defaults use the published Databricks companion tints
  exactly (Create hover `#1D619C`, Share border `#C6C9D1`, Share hover
  `#F0F1F3`); custom colours derive them (Create hover = 14% toward black,
  Share border = 76% toward white, Share hover = 6% tint on white, on-Create
  label = WCAG-safe black or white).
- The backend derives them in `back.core.helpers.UIBranding.derive_button_palette`;
  `settings.js` mirrors that derivation for the live preview and must stay
  numerically identical.
- The server injects `--db-btn-primary`, `--db-btn-primary-hover`,
  `--db-btn-primary-on`, `--db-btn-secondary`, `--db-btn-secondary-border`
  and `--db-btn-secondary-hover` through `#uiBrandingTokens`. `main.css`
  ships the same defaults in `:root`.
- Mapping: `.btn-primary` = **Create** (solid `--db-btn-primary`, no gradient,
  no resting shadow). Every other Bootstrap action variant = **Share**
  (`.btn-secondary`, `.btn-outline-secondary`, `.btn-outline-primary`,
  `.btn-success`, `.btn-warning`, `.btn-info`, `.btn-light`, `.btn-dark` and
  their outline forms): white `--db-surface-warm` at rest, `--db-btn-secondary-border`
  1px border, `--db-btn-secondary` label, `--db-btn-secondary-hover` on hover.
  Class names may stay semantic for JS/intent; the paint is Share.
- Destructive exception: `.btn-danger` and `.btn-outline-danger` keep the Share
  structure with danger colours (`--db-status-danger`). `.btn-link` stays
  text-only; `.btn-close` is unchanged.
- This covers page CTAs, modal footers, section headers, and Studio / graph
  toolbars. Navbar chrome (Save / Close / Resume) uses the Genie chip style
  instead — see Unified app chrome below.
- Geometry is Du Bois md for every `.btn` / `.btn-sm` / `.btn-lg`: height
  `--db-btn-height` (32px), radius `--db-btn-radius` (4px), font
  `--db-btn-font-size` / `--db-btn-font-weight` (13px / 500), horizontal
  padding `--db-btn-padding-x`. Navbar chrome reuses the same tokens.
  `.btn-link` and `.btn-close` stay out of that ramp. Form fields keep
  `--db-radius-control` (2px); only buttons use `--db-btn-radius`.

### Accent Use and Button Hierarchy

The Clarity indigo accent (`--db-primary`) is used for high-signal moments:

- L1/L2 active navigation and active/hover emphasis.
- Focus states and control focus ring (`--db-focus-ring`).
- Active indicators (sidebar stripe, square selected tabs with indigo-soft
  fill, selected controls).
- Primary emphasis utilities (`.badge-primary`, `.bg-primary`, `.text-primary`, `.border-primary`).

Button hierarchy in `global/css/components.css` is mandatory:

- `.btn-primary` (Create) is the only **filled primary** action and should be the
  rightmost section CTA.
- Every other non-danger action variant is **Share** (outlined on the white
  surface with the secondary button tokens) — including `.btn-outline-primary`
  and the solid/outline success/warning/info/light/dark classes.
- `.btn-danger` / `.btn-outline-danger` are the semantic destructive exception
  (Share structure, red text/border, soft-danger hover), never a filled CTA.
- Status/destructive intent cannot rely on color alone; include icon + label.

### Typography

The system font stack is mandatory across app-shell pages.

- System font stack only:
  `-apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, "Segoe UI", Roboto, "Helvetica Neue", Arial, sans-serif`.
- No webfont imports. No `@font-face`. No Google Fonts.
- OntoViz uses `--ovz-font-main`, `--ovz-font-display`, `--ovz-font-mono`
  defined in `ontoviz-variables.css`.
- Body default size is `13.5px`, with compact app chrome (`0.8125rem`
  navbar/subnav links) and strong page titles (`.page-header h1` = `2rem`,
  `700`, slight tracking).
- Keep the scale consistent: page title > section title (`h4`) > body/labels >
  helper/muted text.

### Semantic Status Vocabulary

Use semantic tinted status vocabulary consistently across badges, alerts, chips,
inline notices, and table states:

- `success`: `--db-status-success` + `--db-status-success-soft-bg`
- `warning`: `--db-status-warning` + `--db-status-warning-soft-bg`
- `danger`: `--db-status-danger` + `--db-status-danger-soft-bg`
- `info`: `--db-status-info` + `--db-status-info-soft-bg`
- `draft`: `--db-status-draft` + `--db-status-draft-soft-bg`

Status color is assistive, not sufficient alone: provide icon + label text.

### Surface, Borders, Radius, Elevation

- Resting component borders are one-pixel and warm (`--db-border` /
  `--db-border-light`).
- Shared shape scale is mandatory: `2px` cards, `2px` controls/buttons, `2px`
  chips (`--db-radius-chip`).
- Resting cards/forms/tables keep `box-shadow: none`; elevation is reserved for
  floating surfaces (dropdowns, modals, overlays, transient toasts).
- Tables/forms/tabs/modals/dropdowns must use warm/white surfaces and warm
  separators from the shared token set; avoid isolated per-page palette drift.

### Iconography

- **Bootstrap Icons** only (`<i class="bi bi-..."></i>`), loaded from CDN in
  `base.html`.
- Menu/sidebar icons are configured **declaratively** in
  `src/front/config/menu_config.json` (`"icon": "bi-..."`), never hard-coded
  in templates.
- Do not introduce another icon font, SVG icon library, or emoji-as-icon.
- Keep existing OntoBricks logo and favicon assets (`#brandLogoImg`,
  `global/img/favicon.svg`) as-is; no replacement logo set.

### Page Layout (mandatory skeleton)

Every page **must** extend `templates/base.html`. The base template owns
the entire app chrome — never duplicate or substitute any of it:

| Element | Provided by `base.html` | Don't reimplement |
|---------|-------------------------|-------------------|
| Unified app chrome | `<nav id="obSubnav" class="ob-subnav navbar navbar-light">` — always visible; left = sidebar toggle + brand; right = Context crumb, Close → Save, app switcher (`#obAppSwitcher`), Activity, User | a second top bar or page-local chrome |
| Save Domain button | `#menuSaveDomain` (`.ob-subnav-save-btn`) in the right side of `#obSubnav`, with dirty dot and shortcut keycap | a per-page save button |
| Domain loading overlay | `#domainLoadingOverlay` / `.domain-loading-overlay` | another full-page spinner |
| Activity Center dropdown | `#activityCenterDropdown` (`bi-inboxes`) | a per-page tasks + notifications panel |
| Help Center modal | included via `partials/layout/help_modal.html` | a custom help dialog |
| Workspace Map modal | included via `partials/layout/_workspace_map_modal.html`, opened from `#domainContextTrigger`; header top-right has Save/Close (`data-navbar-action`) above the version switch strip | a duplicate workspace launcher |
| UC File Browser modal | included via `partials/layout/uc_file_browser_modal.html` | another file picker |
| Body data attributes | `data-app-role`, `data-domain-role`, `data-app-mode` | re-fetching them |
| Bootstrap JS bundle | loaded after `{% block content %}` | re-loading Bootstrap |

A page template **must** look like this (and nothing more at the top
level):

```html
{% extends "base.html" %}

{% block title %}<Page Name> - OntoBricks{% endblock %}

{% block extra_css %}
<link rel="stylesheet" href="{{ url_for('static', filename='<area>/css/<area>-<feature>.css') }}?v={{ asset_version }}">
{% endblock %}

{% block content %}
   ...one of the two layout patterns below...
{% endblock %}

{% block extra_js %}
<script src="{{ url_for('static', filename='<area>/js/<area>-<feature>.js') }}?v={{ asset_version }}"></script>
{% endblock %}
```

There are exactly **two** acceptable layouts inside `{% block content %}`.
Pick one, do not invent a third.

#### Layout 1 — Sidebar layout (default for app pages)

Used by the five sidebar page families: `domain.html`, `ontology.html`,
`mapping.html`, `dtwin.html`, `settings.html`. This is the default for any
page that exposes more than one functional section. Registry browse is a
modal, not a sixth sidebar family.

```html
<div class="container-fluid px-0">
    <div class="sidebar-layout">
        <!-- Left sidebar - rendered from menu_config.json -->
        <div class="sidebar-nav">
            {% set sidebar_menu = get_menu('<area>') %}
            {% include "partials/layout/_sidebar_nav.html" %}
        </div>

        <!-- Right content area: one section per sidebar item -->
        <div class="sidebar-content">
            <div id="information-section" class="sidebar-section active">
                {% include "partials/<area>/_<area>_information.html" %}
            </div>
            <div id="design-section" class="sidebar-section">
                {% include "partials/<area>/_<area>_design.html" %}
            </div>
            <!-- ...one <div id="<id>-section" class="sidebar-section"> per menu item... -->
        </div>
    </div>
</div>
```

Rules for sidebar pages:

- The active section is a Databricks-style **white page panel** (title,
  subtitle and body on white, 1px border). Under `.section-header`, wrap
  the rest of the section in `<div class="card"><div class="card-body">…</div></div>`
  (or an equivalent `.card` workspace such as Versions /
  `ob-tabs-wrap`). Do not leave tiles, tables, forms, or empty states
  floating outside a card. Reference: Domain → Versions
  (good) vs a bare Validation layout (bad).
- The sidebar **must** be the `_sidebar_nav.html` partial driven by
  `menu_config.json` — never hand-code `<nav class="sidebar-nav">` items.
- Section ids are `<menu-item-id>-section`. The id must match the
  `data-section` attribute emitted by the partial for the
  section-switcher to pick it up (`sidebar-nav.js`).
- Exactly **one** section carries `.active` on initial render (typically
  the menu item flagged `default: true`, or the section name passed via
  `?section=...` querystring).
- Each section's body lives in a partial under
  `templates/partials/<area>/_<area>_<feature>.html`. Page templates
  never inline the section bodies.
- `.content-wrapper` constraints are intentionally overridden when a
  `.sidebar-layout` is present (see `sidebar-layout.css`) — full width,
  zero wrapper padding. Desktop viewport height is owned by
  `.sidebar-layout` via `--ob-chrome-height`. Do not re-add `padding` or
  `max-width` on the wrapper, and do not give descendant panes their own
  `100vh` arithmetic.

#### Layout 2 — Standalone content (single-purpose pages only)

Used by `home.html`, `about.html`, `access_denied.html`. Reserve for
landing/info pages that do not need a sidebar.

```html
{# Content sits inside base.html's default .content-wrapper:
   max-width: 1400px, padding: 2rem, auto-centred #}
<div class="page-header">
    <h1><i class="bi bi-..."></i> Page Title</h1>
    <p>Optional subtitle in --db-text-secondary.</p>
</div>

<!-- ...page content... -->
```

Rules for standalone pages:

- Use `.page-header` for the title block — do not invent another header
  treatment.
- Stay inside the default `.content-wrapper`. Do not wrap the content in
  `.container-fluid` or override the wrapper's max-width.

### Sidebar Behaviour

The sidebar is a self-contained component owned by
`global/js/sidebar-nav.js` + `global/css/sidebar-layout.css`. Page code
**must not** reimplement section switching, collapse, URL sync or
keyboard handling.

**Auto-init contract**

- `sidebar-nav.js` auto-initialises on `DOMContentLoaded` whenever a
  `.sidebar-nav` exists. To opt out (extremely rare), set
  `window.SIDEBAR_NAV_MANUAL_INIT = true` *before* the script runs and
  call `SidebarNav.init({...})` yourself.
- Each nav item must carry `data-section="<id>"` and the corresponding
  content block must have id `"<id>-section"` and class
  `"sidebar-section"`. Mismatch = silent no-op.
- Exactly one `.nav-link` and one `.sidebar-section` carry `.active` at
  any time. Never set `display: none / block` directly on a section —
  the `.active` class is what the CSS reads.

**State, history and keyboard**

- The active section is mirrored to `?section=<id>` in the URL via
  `history.pushState`, and restored from query string or hash on load
  and on `popstate` (Back / Forward work).
- Collapsed/expanded state is persisted in `localStorage` under
  `ontobricks-sidebar-collapsed`. **Default state is collapsed** — do
  not change this default. Preference is applied before first paint by
  synchronous `global/js/sidebar-collapse-init.js` (sets
  `html.ob-sidebar-collapsed`); `sidebar-nav.js` keeps `.sidebar-nav.collapsed`
  in sync. Do not remove the pre-paint script or reintroduce a flash of
  the expanded rail on load.
- The collapse toggle is a **fixed chrome control** (`#obSidebarToggle`,
  `.ob-subnav-sidebar-toggle`) placed left of the brand (and still
  visible on Settings, left of the return link). `SidebarNav._setupCollapse()`
  reveals it when a `.sidebar-nav` exists and removes any legacy in-rail
  `.sidebar-toggle`. Do not inject a toggle into the sidebar. It has no
  hover highlight (no `:hover` background); do not add one.
- A `sidebarSectionChanged` `CustomEvent` (`detail: { section,
  targetSection }`) is dispatched on `document` after every switch.
  This is the official extension point — listen to it instead of
  patching click handlers.

**Public API (use these, not raw DOM manipulation)**

| Call | Purpose |
|------|---------|
| `SidebarNav.switchTo(name)` | Programmatically activate a section |
| `SidebarNav.getActiveSection()` | Read the current section id |
| `SidebarNav.toggleCollapse()` | Programmatically collapse/expand |
| `SidebarNav.init({ onSectionChange, onBeforeSectionChange })` | Re-init with hooks; `onBeforeSectionChange` may return `false` (or a Promise resolving to `false`) to veto the switch |

**Style rules**

- Sidebar width: `200px` expanded, `48px` collapsed (driven by
  `.sidebar-nav.collapsed`). Do not override per page.
- The sidebar has no background (`background: transparent`) and no border
  (`border: none`), matching the unified chrome bar (`#obSubnav`):
  `margin: 0`, `height: 100%`. The **shell** owns the compact outer
  gutter (`.sidebar-layout { padding: 0.15rem 0.25rem 0.25rem; gap: 0.25rem; }`); do not
  put a margin on `.sidebar-nav` or size it with
  `calc(100% - 1rem)`. Don't reintroduce the `border-right`-only
  hairline. See *Desktop geometry, scroll, and bottom alignment*.
- Nav-link visual: `0.82rem`, left-border accent of `3px transparent`
  slot with rounded row targets and indigo-soft hover
  (`--db-hover-indigo`). Active state uses a stronger indigo-soft
  background (`--db-primary-light` at higher opacity), indigo text/icon
  (`--db-primary`), and a `3px` active indicator. The same hover/active
  pair is shared by L1 and L2 dropdown items.
- Section group titles (`.sidebar-title`) are uppercase `0.65rem`,
  `letter-spacing: 1px`, muted token text. Hidden when collapsed.
- Icons inside `.nav-link i` are `0.95rem`, fixed `20px` slot
  (alignment is consistent across the menu).
- Disabled gates: `.sidebar-disabled` (generic),
  `.sidebar-requires-domain` (no domain loaded),
  `.sync-requires-ready` (triplestore empty), `.view-mode-hidden`
  (read-only viewer). Use these classes — do not invent new ones, and
  do not toggle them per page; they're driven by `permissions.js` and
  the `data-domain-saved` body attributes.
- Badges inside a nav-link are auto-wrapped into the `.nav-label`
  span by the collapse logic and hidden when collapsed.
- When the sidebar is collapsed, hover tooltips (`placement: right`,
  `trigger: hover`, `container: body`) indicate the feature name via
  Bootstrap Tooltip, powered by `data-sidebar-title="{{ item.label }}"` on
  each link. When expanded, tooltips are disabled so they do not duplicate
  the visible label.

**Don'ts**

- ❌ Hand-coding `<a class="nav-link">` items in the page template —
  the menu is rendered by `partials/layout/_sidebar_nav.html` from
  `menu_config.json`.
- ❌ Calling `addEventListener('click', ...)` on `.nav-link` items to
  intercept switching — use `onBeforeSectionChange` /
  `sidebarSectionChanged`.
- ❌ Toggling `style.display` on `.sidebar-section` directly.
- ❌ Persisting your own per-page sidebar state — there's one
  `localStorage` key for the whole app.
- ❌ Adjusting `--ob-chrome-height` from page code — the breadcrumb
  computes it.
- ❌ Pane-level `100vh` / `calc(100vh - Npx)` on canvases, lists, or
  consoles. Only `.sidebar-layout` keys height off the viewport.
- ❌ Extra bottom padding on `.sidebar-content` or a fixed pane that
  shortens the surface relative to the menu.

### Desktop geometry, scroll, and bottom alignment

Canonical source: `documentation/superpowers/specs/2026-08-26-sidebar-bottom-alignment-design.md`
and `global/css/sidebar-layout.css`.

**Shell.** On desktop, `.sidebar-layout` is
`height` / `max-height: calc(100vh - var(--ob-chrome-height, 60px))`,
`padding: 0.25rem 0.5rem 0.5rem`, `gap: 0.5rem`, `box-sizing: border-box`,
`overflow: hidden`. `--ob-chrome-height` is the single `#obSubnav` bar height,
written only by `breadcrumb.js`.

**Gutter.** `.ob-subnav-nav` owns vertical padding inside the chrome bar
(`padding: 0.15rem 0`); `.sidebar-layout` owns the matching space below the
bar. Therefore the visible chrome→page gap is ~4px. `.sidebar-nav` fills the shell (`margin: 0; height: 100%`), while
`.sidebar-content` uses `padding: 0 0.5rem`: no second vertical inset, and a
0.5rem horizontal inset. Page-specific fixed panes may change horizontal
padding but must keep a zero top/bottom inset. Do not restore vertical content
padding that shifts the title below the sidebar or shortens the pane.
Every `.sidebar-layout .section-header` uses `padding-top: 0.25rem` and
`margin-bottom: 0.25rem !important`, so the space above the title equals the
space below the subtitle, identically across sections (including Ontology
Studio and Business Views). The top inset must not be zeroed on desktop.

**Flex chain.** `.sidebar-content` is a column flex container with
`min-height: 0`. `.sidebar-section.active` is `flex: 1 1 0; min-height: 0`.
Direct-child `.content-section` continues the chain (`flex: 1; min-height: 0`).
Primary surfaces (cards, split shells, tab wraps) stretch through that
chain. True modals stay siblings of the stretch root.

**Scroll ownership.** Default / long-form pages scroll in
`.sidebar-content` (`overflow-y: auto`). Fixed-height panes (Explorer,
Query, Data Quality, Logs) hide overflow on the content shell and scroll
inside a dedicated owner (canvas, GraphiQL, DQ tab body,
`#logsConsoleWrap` in `config.css` — not inline style). Alignment is the
main surface vs the menu, not stuffing every form field into one viewport.

**Rendered invariant.** At 1600×1000, `.sidebar-nav` and the visible
primary pane share `getBoundingClientRect().bottom` within **1px**.
The visible section title box (`.section-header h4`, including its icon) sits
**0.25rem (4px ±1px)** below the sidebar content top — the same gap as subtitle
→ content. Desktop headers must not use a negative transform. The
visible chrome→sidebar gap is **2.5px ±1px**. The gap from subtitle
to primary content is **4px ±1px**.
Fixed-height routes must not grow the document past the viewport by more
than 1px.

**Mobile.** Below `768px` the shell returns to normal document flow:
`height: auto`, `max-height: none`, `overflow: visible`, including
specificity-matched `:has()` resets. Do not keep a pane at `100vh` or
`overflow: hidden` on narrow viewports. Knowledge Graph Data Quality must
reset `#dataquality-section.active` to `height: auto; overflow: visible`
after its desktop rule. Graph Chat must likewise reset the same desktop
selector, `#chat-section.sidebar-section.active`, to
`height: auto; overflow: visible` in a later mobile rule.
For the compact L2 domain identity on this breakpoint, the leading icon must
return to normal flow (`position: static`, no translate transform) and the
trigger keeps explicit icon-control sizing (`width: auto`, bounded min width,
and `0.5rem` horizontal padding) so `scrollWidth <= clientWidth` remains true.

**Excluded.** Home, About, and Access Denied stay on Layout 2. True
modals are not the measured pane.

### Unified App Chrome (`#obSubnav`)

The app chrome is **one horizontal bar** provided by `base.html`
(`<nav id="obSubnav" class="ob-subnav navbar navbar-light">`). Former L1 and
L2 are merged; do not restore a second top nav.

- Always visible. Brand, Activity, and User never hide with domain state
  (on Home, the Activity button is not necessary and is hidden via CSS).
- Domain chrome toggles via `updateDomainMenuVisibility(hasDomain)` in
  `navbar.js` (elements marked `data-subnav-domain-chrome`):
  - **Workspace page + loaded domain:** show Context crumb, Close/Save, app switcher.
  - **Settings/Home + loaded domain:** show `#settingsDomainReturnLink` only
    (hide workspace domain chrome).
  - **No loaded domain:** hide domain chrome and the settings return.
- User control is a Databricks-style initials avatar (`.ob-user-avatar`), not
  `bi-person-circle`. Settings (admin-only, `#navSettingsLink` with a separator
  underneath), Help, and Report an issue live inside the user dropdown. No gear
  in chrome and no Settings row in `#obAppSwitcher`.
- Visual contract: transparent bar over the canvas grey (no bottom border),
  dark text, indigo hover/active emphasis.

**Top-level workspace icons** (source of truth: `menu_config.json`):

| Menu | `id` | Icon |
|------|------|------|
| Registry | `registry` | `bi-boxes` |
| Domain | `domain` | `bi-box` |
| Ontology | `ontology` | `bi-bezier2` |
| Mapping | `assignment` | `bi-shuffle` |
| Graph | `digitaltwin` | `bi-radar` |
| Intelligence | `intelligence` | `bi-sliders2-vertical` |
| Settings | `settings` | `bi-gear` (user dropdown only) |

Do not reuse Domain's `bi-box` for Graph (or any other top-level menu).
`tests/units/front/test_knowledge_graph_icon.py` asserts Domain ≠ Graph.

**Layout (left → right inside `.ob-subnav-nav`):**

1. **Sidebar collapse toggle** (`#obSidebarToggle`, fixed — not under
   `data-subnav-domain-chrome`): revealed only when the page has a
   `.sidebar-nav`. Sits left of the brand.
2. **Brand** (`#brandLogoImg` + `#brandTitleText` + optional app version):
   links to `menu_config.brand.route`.
3. **Settings/Home return** (`#settingsDomainReturnNav`, mutually exclusive
   with domain chrome): aligned on the right, just before Activity, shown on
   Settings/Home when a domain is loaded.
4. **`.ob-subnav-flex-spacer`** (`flex: 1`) — pushes the right group to the edge.
5. **Domain crumb** (`#domainContextTrigger`, `.ob-subnav-crumb`, domain chrome):
   domain name, optional status badge, version badge (`#domainContextVersion`),
   and caret. Opens `#workspaceMapModal` (also hosts Switch Version / New Version).
6. **Close** (`.ob-subnav-close-btn`) then **Save** (`#menuSaveDomain`,
   `.ob-subnav-save-btn`) — domain chrome. See action-styles below.
7. **App switcher** (`#obAppSwitcher`, `.ob-subnav-app-switcher`): Databricks-style
   9-dot `nav-icon` after Close/Save. Opens `.ob-app-switcher-menu` as a vertical
   list (icon + title + subtitle + check) for Context / Ontology / Graph /
   Intelligence. Each item opens that workspace's **Home** (`?section=home`).
   Graph + Intelligence carry `nav-requires-graph`. Current workspace gets
   `.is-current` via `initAppSwitcherActiveState()`. Popover min-width `22rem`;
   menu/items use rounded boxes (`0.5rem` / `0.375rem`). Workspace Home tiles
   follow the Databricks MCP card pattern in `_workspace_home.html`.
8. **Settings/Home return link** (`#settingsDomainReturnNav`): when active on
   Settings/Home, aligned on the right immediately preceding Activity.
9. **Activity** (`#activityCenterToggle`) then **User** (`#userMenuDropdown`).
   Settings is the admin-gated `#navSettingsLink` inside the user dropdown
   (`data-requires-app="admin"`), not an app-switcher row.

- The old segmented workspace dropdown rail and the separate L1 top navbar are
  **removed**. Do not restore them. Per-section navigation stays in each page's
  left sidebar; the app switcher is the only workspace navigator.
- `--ob-chrome-height` = `#obSubnav` height only. Updated by
  `_updateChromeHeight()` in `breadcrumb.js` (also called from
  `updateDomainMenuVisibility`).
- Active-state behavior for workspaces is owned by `initAppSwitcherActiveState()`
  (called from `initSubnavActiveState()`). Do not hard-code the active workspace
  in Jinja and do not duplicate this behavior in page scripts.
- Domain crumb is text (`--db-text`, weight 600; hover `--db-primary-darker` on
  `--db-hover-indigo`). Name ellipsized (`max-width: 10rem`); rail height stays
  `2.25rem`.
- Left edge aligns with the sidebar panel: `.sidebar-layout` padding and
  `#obSubnav .container-fluid { padding-left: 0.25rem !important; }` (ID
  specificity required against legacy `!important` zeroing). Right padding
  stays `0.5rem`.
- Keyboard focus uses `--db-focus-ring` plus a transparent outline for forced-colour support.
- Action styles: Close, Save, and Resume share `.ob-subnav-action` as Genie
  Code-inspired chips (Du Bois md: 32px / `--ob-subnav-btn-height`, 4px /
  `--ob-subnav-btn-radius`, 13px weight 500): white surface, 1px `--db-border`,
  secondary label/icon. Hover uses the same fill as Activity
  (`--db-hover-indigo`) — never a gradient and never the Create/Share fills.
  Save and Close are **icon-only** (visible label removed; `title` + `aria-label`
  required): bare at rest, and on hover they show the same `--ob-subnav-icon-hit`
  (2rem) / `--ob-subnav-icon-radius` square as Activity and the feature switcher,
  filled with `--db-hover-indigo`. Resume is **text-only** (`Resume`, no icon),
  warning-coloured. Save keeps its dirty dot (`.ob-subnav-save-dirty`). No
  shortcut keycap.
  Rendered right-side order: Context → Close → Save → app switcher → Activity →
  User. Preserve existing IDs, permission classes, and handlers.
- Shared ownership: markup in `templates/base.html`, styling in
  `static/global/css/main.css`, behavior in `static/global/js/navbar.js`, menus
  from `menu_config.json`. Never add per-page chrome CSS or JS.
- Stable IDs: `#settingsDomainReturnNav`, `#settingsDomainReturnLink`,
  `#obAppSwitcher`. The settings return control sits on the right side of the chrome
  bar immediately preceding Activity Center (`#activityCenterToggle`).
  The label is never truncated (natural width, `white-space: nowrap`).
- Mobile contract (below `768px`): keep app switcher, Save, and Close as titled
  icons by hiding `.ob-subnav-label`. Hide the flex spacer,
  `.ob-subnav-context-copy`, and the save keycap. Context collapses to its caret.
  Do not make the bar horizontally scrollable.

**Tokens**

| Level | Token | Value |
|-------|-------|-------|
| Chrome bar background | transparent (canvas grey shows through) | no bottom border |
| App switcher current row | `--db-primary-light` / `--db-primary-darker` | `.ob-app-switcher-item.is-current` (+ visible check) |
| Context crumb | `--db-text` / `--db-hover-indigo` | quiet text crumb on the chrome bar |
| Save dirty dot | `--db-status-warning` | `.ob-subnav-save-dirty`, shown only when dirty |

**New Domain flow**

`domainNew()` in `navbar.js`:
1. Opens `showNewDomainDialog()` (from `utils.js`) — collects name,
   description, LLM endpoint.
2. POSTs `/domain/clear` then `/domain/info`.
3. Immediately opens `showDomainSaveDialog({ afterSave: '/domain/#information' })`
   so the domain is registered in UC before navigation.

`showNewDomainDialog()` is exposed as `window.showNewDomainDialog`.

**Don'ts**

- ❌ Adding a third nav level above or below these two.
- ❌ Toggling `#obSubnav` visibility from page code — it is controlled
  exclusively by `updateDomainMenuVisibility` in `navbar.js`.
- ❌ Restoring the L2 workspace dropdown rail
  (`.ob-subnav-workspaces` / `#subnavDomainDropdown` / etc.) beside the
  Context crumb — use `#obAppSwitcher` only.
- ❌ Restoring a separate L1 top navbar above `#obSubnav`
- ❌ Restoring a full-width second-row background, active underline,
  square selected tab or horizontally scrollable strip.
- ❌ Giving L2 a different left gutter from `.sidebar-layout`; the L2 row
  and sidebar panel must remain vertically aligned.
- ❌ Filling Save/Close with Create/Share colours, or a hover gradient on
  chrome actions. Chrome chips stay bordered neutrals; hover is Activity's
  `--db-hover-indigo` only.
- ❌ Hard-coding `.is-current` / `aria-current` on app-switcher items in
  Jinja or page code — `navbar.js` owns that state.
- ❌ Restoring a 2×2 tile grid for the app switcher — keep the Databricks
  line-item list (`.ob-app-switcher-list` / `.ob-app-switcher-item`).
- ❌ Moving the Save Domain button out of `#obSubnav`.
- ❌ Manually setting `--ob-chrome-height` from page code.

### Breadcrumb (removed)

The inline L2 breadcrumb was removed (Nov 2026). Do not reintroduce a
breadcrumb trail in the L2 row or below the navbar; the active workspace is
already shown by the workspace track, and the section by the sidebar.

`global/js/breadcrumb.js` now only keeps `--ob-chrome-height` in sync and
exposes `window.OBBreadcrumb._updateChromeHeight()` for `navbar.js`.

**Don'ts**

- ❌ Adding a breadcrumb trail to the L2 row or any page header.
- ❌ Setting `--ob-chrome-height` from page code (see above).

### Section Body Surface (Databricks page panel + card under the title)

Canonical reference: **Domain → Versions** (`partials/domain/_domain_versions.html`).
Like Databricks product pages, the active sidebar section
(`.sidebar-section.active`) is **one white panel** with a 1px `--db-border`
border, only the **top-left corner rounded** (`--ob-page-panel-radius`, 0.5rem,
declared on `.sidebar-layout`; the other corners stay square) and
`padding: var(--ob-page-padding)` (also declared on `.sidebar-layout`). Title, subtitle and body all sit on that
white panel; only the sidebar and the outer gutter stay on the warm canvas.
Full-height sections that override padding by ID (`#logs-section`,
`#design-section`, `#map-section`, `#sigmagraph-section`, `#graphql-section`)
must reuse `var(--ob-page-padding)`.

Typography (`.section-header`): title `h1`–`h4` 1.375rem (22px), weight 600,
1.75rem line, `--db-text`; title icon 0.85em in `--db-text-secondary`;
subtitle `p` 0.8125rem (13px) in `--db-text-secondary`. The header has no
top padding (the panel owns it) and a 0.75rem gap to the body.

Inside the panel every section follows the same two-zone layout:

1. **Title zone.** `.content-section` holds one
   `.section-header` (title, subtitle, top-right actions). Nothing else
   sits in the title zone.
2. **Body zone — on a bordered card.** Everything below the header lives in one
   `.card` (or an equivalent `.card` workspace such as `.card h-100` +
   `ob-tabs-wrap`), with `.card-body` for padding when the content is not
   already a card-integrated workspace.

Skeleton:

```html
<div class="content-section">
    <div class="section-header d-flex justify-content-between align-items-center mb-4">
        <div>
            <h4 class="mb-1"><i class="bi bi-<icon> me-2"></i>Section Title</h4>
            <p class="text-muted mb-0 small">One-line description.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="d-flex gap-2"><!-- actions --></div>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
        <div class="card-body">
            <!-- tiles, tables, forms, empty / loading states -->
        </div>
    </div>
</div>
```

Rules:

- Never leave body content (tiles, tables, forms, placeholders, spinners,
  empty states) directly under `.section-header` outside a card.
  Validation before the fix is the counter-example.
- Do not paint `.sidebar-content` white or add a second page border: the
  page panel belongs to `.sidebar-section.active` only.
- Do not nest a second white card around a body that already is a card
  (Versions, `_domain_metadata`, Ontology Studio split shells). Full-height
  split panes keep their existing `.card` / `ob-split-shell` structure.
- The sidebar itself (`.sidebar-nav`) stays transparent.
- Contract tests: `tests/units/front/test_section_content_white_panel.py`,
  `tests/units/front/test_databricks_page_panel_contract.py`.

### Section / Partial Conventions

- One partial per section. Filename:
  `templates/partials/<area>/_<area>_<feature>.html` (note the leading
  underscore — it marks it as a non-route partial).
- A section partial **never** includes `<link>` or `<script>` tags. All
  CSS/JS is wired in the parent page's `extra_css` / `extra_js` blocks.
- A section partial may include sub-partials but **must not** redefine
  the section wrapper (`<div id="...-section" class="sidebar-section">`)
  — that lives in the page template.

### Section Header & Top-Right Actions

Every section starts with a **`.section-header`** row that pairs a
title block on the left with action buttons on the right. This is the
single allowed pattern for page-level actions — actions never live at
the bottom of a section, never float on top of the content, and never
hide inside a Bootstrap `.card-header`.

**Canonical skeleton**

```html
<div class="section-header d-flex justify-content-between align-items-center mb-4">
    <div>
        <h4 class="mb-1"><i class="bi bi-<icon> me-2"></i>Section Title</h4>
        <p class="text-muted mb-0 small">One-line description of what this section does.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="d-flex gap-2">
        <!-- Utility / icon-only buttons first (refresh, expand/collapse, settings cog) -->
        <div class="btn-group btn-group-sm">
            <button type="button" class="btn btn-outline-secondary" title="Refresh">
                <i class="bi bi-arrow-clockwise"></i>
            </button>
        </div>
        <!-- Secondary CTAs next, primary CTA last (rightmost) -->
        <button type="button" class="btn btn-sm btn-outline-secondary">
            <i class="bi bi-cloud-download me-1"></i> Load
        </button>
        <button type="button" class="btn btn-sm btn-primary">
            <i class="bi bi-plus-circle me-1"></i> New
        </button>
    </div>
</div>
```

**Rules**

- Action buttons **must** sit in the **top-right** of the section, in
  the right-hand `<div>` of `.section-header`. No exceptions: this is
  the location users learn once and look for everywhere.
- Use `btn-sm` (small). Section headers carry a lot of buttons — full-
  size `.btn` makes them shout.
- Order from left to right: **icon-only utility buttons → secondary
  CTAs → primary CTA**. The rightmost button is the most important
  action of the section.
- Group icon-only utility buttons (refresh, expand all, collapse all,
  settings) inside a `.btn-group.btn-group-sm` so they read as a
  cluster.
- Every icon-only button **must** have a `title=` attribute (and ideally
  `aria-label=`) — it is the only label the user sees.
- Use Bootstrap colour intent (`btn-primary`, `btn-outline-secondary`,
  `btn-success`, `btn-danger`) to communicate hierarchy/semantics.
  `.btn-primary` is visibly filled; secondary and non-danger outlines stay
  unfilled/neutral; destructive outline may use semantic red.
- Buttons with a label start with a Bootstrap icon followed by
  `me-1`, e.g. `<i class="bi bi-plus-circle me-1"></i> Add Entity`.
- Title `<h4>` uses `mb-1`; subtitle `<p>` uses `text-muted mb-0 small`.
  Don't introduce `<h2>`/`<h3>` inside a sidebar section — `.h4` is the
  level for section titles (page-level `<h1>` belongs to
  `.page-header` on standalone pages only).
- The whole row uses `mb-4`. The next sibling is the section's main
  card / content. Don't add a `<hr>` between them — the `mb-4` is the
  separator.

**Anti-patterns**

- ❌ A floating "+" FAB (Material-style) — use the top-right button.
- ❌ Action buttons placed below the data they act on.
- ❌ `<button>` inside `.card-header` of the section's main card —
  wrong location, wrong styling, wrong size.
- ❌ Full-size buttons in a section header.
- ❌ Buttons without an icon, or icon-only buttons without `title=`.
- ❌ Reordering so the primary CTA is on the left.
- ❌ A utility shortcut (the `.onto-discuss-btn` chat icon, a refresh) placed
  *after* the primary CTA. The rightmost slot belongs to the CTA.
- ❌ `style="display:none"` as the initial state of a button. Use `d-none`
  and have the JS toggle the class, so the button keeps one styling source.
- ❌ A button that a permission rule *hides* (`permissions.css`, as opposed to
  merely neutralising it) placed inside a `.btn-group`: the group rounds its
  corners structurally, so a hidden member leaves its neighbour squared.
  Keep those buttons standalone.

**States**

`.btn-primary` (Create) is the only variant that fills. Everything else is
Share (or danger-Share): white surface at rest, hover tints the surface,
active keeps the hover tint, and focus keeps the resting surface and shows
`--db-focus-ring` alone. Bootstrap's own `:focus-visible` repaints a button
with its *hover* palette, which for these variants is the solid fill we
dropped, so `components.css` pins both `:focus` and `:focus-visible`. When you
add a variant, add it to that list too. `.btn-outline-danger` is the single
exception that keeps its red lettering when focused.

**Responsive behaviour**

`.section-header` and any flex cluster inside it wrap (`main.css`), and below
768px a `.btn-group` inside a header stops being joined: its buttons become
individually rounded, `white-space: nowrap` pills flowing onto as many rows as
they need. A joined group cannot survive wrapping — Bootstrap lets its members
shrink instead, which breaks labels mid-word into buttons three lines tall.

**Radii**

Buttons and fields use `--db-radius-control`. Bootstrap's `-sm` size ramp sets
`border-radius` as a *property* with enough specificity to beat the base rule,
so `main.css` re-pins `.form-control-sm`, `.form-select-sm` and every
`.input-group-sm > *` to the token — otherwise small joined controls silently
fall back to Bootstrap's legacy 4px next to their full-size siblings.

### Tabs (`.ob-tabs`) — transparent underline rail

There is **one** tab-navigation treatment across the application. It lives in
`global/css/components.css` and follows the Databricks product tabs (such as
Computes / Serverless): a transparent, square rail with a continuous 1px
baseline header line (`border-bottom: 1px solid var(--db-border)`), left-aligned
13px labels spaced `1.5rem` apart (`gap`), and links with **no horizontal padding**
so the active underline is exactly as wide as the label. Inactive labels use
`--db-text-secondary` at weight 500. The active item keeps a transparent
background, uses `--db-text` at weight 600, and draws a **3px** `--db-primary`
underline sitting on top of the baseline border (`margin-bottom: -1px`). The
rail has no outer fill, card outline, shadow, or radius.

Hover only recolours the label to `--db-primary` (follows the Settings
branding colour); there is **no hover fill**. Keyboard focus draws a 2px
`--db-primary` outline offset by 2px.

Inside the page panel, enclosing workspace cards (`.sidebar-section > .content-section > .card`,
`form.card`, `.card:has(.ob-tabs-wrap)`, `.card.ob-tabs-wrap`) have no border,
shadow or card background, so there is never an enclosing box/border under the
section title/sub-title; tabs and page content sit directly on the panel surface
flush with the section header.

Every tab navigation must carry `nav nav-tabs ob-tabs`. Use
`ob-tabs--compact` only for constrained right-hand panels. Do not introduce
boxed Bootstrap tabs, pills, active fills, rounded selected items, or per-area
visual states.

The rail owns single-line responsive behaviour: `flex-wrap: nowrap`,
`white-space: nowrap`, and horizontal overflow. Labels never wrap or truncate;
users can scroll the rail on narrow screens. Keyboard focus uses a visible 2px
`--db-primary` inset outline and must never be removed.

Choose the container from the visual relationship. Use card-integrated page
tabs when the rail and content form one logical workspace. Use
`.ob-tab-content` only when the content is a genuinely independent bordered,
rounded surface. `.ob-tabs` never owns card borders or corner geometry.

#### Card-integrated page tabs

This is the canonical page-level pattern used by Domain → General,
Ontology → Generate, Ontology → Data Quality, Ontology → Business Rules, and
Ontology → Axioms:

```html
<div class="card h-100">
    <div class="card-body p-0 ob-tabs-wrap">
        <ul class="nav nav-tabs ob-tabs nav-fill"
            id="<unique>Tabs" role="tablist">
            ...
        </ul>
        <div class="tab-content p-3" id="<unique>TabContent">
            ...
        </div>
    </div>
</div>
```

The outer `<div>` may be a `<form class="card h-100">` when one form owns the
whole workspace. Keep the inner `card-body p-0 ob-tabs-wrap`, `nav-fill` rail,
and padded `tab-content` unchanged.

#### Independent tab content

Use the following skeleton only when the rail and body are separate visual
surfaces:

```html
<ul class="nav nav-tabs ob-tabs" id="<unique>Tabs" role="tablist">
    <li class="nav-item" role="presentation">
        <button class="nav-link active"
                id="<id>-tab-foo"
                data-bs-toggle="tab"
                data-bs-target="#<id>-pane-foo"
                type="button" role="tab"
                aria-controls="<id>-pane-foo"
                aria-selected="true">
            <i class="bi bi-<icon> me-1"></i>Foo
        </button>
    </li>
    <li class="nav-item" role="presentation">
        <button class="nav-link"
                id="<id>-tab-bar"
                data-bs-toggle="tab"
                data-bs-target="#<id>-pane-bar"
                type="button" role="tab"
                aria-controls="<id>-pane-bar"
                aria-selected="false">
            <i class="bi bi-<icon> me-1"></i>Bar
        </button>
    </li>
</ul>

<div class="tab-content" id="<id>TabContent">
    <div class="tab-pane fade show active" id="<id>-pane-foo"
         role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="<id>-tab-foo">
        ...
    </div>
    <div class="tab-pane fade" id="<id>-pane-bar"
         role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="<id>-tab-bar">
        ...
    </div>
</div>
```

**Rules**

- The tab `<ul>` **must** carry both `nav nav-tabs` (for Bootstrap
  behaviour) **and** `ob-tabs` (for the visual treatment). The
  `ob-tabs` class is a drop-in addition; Bootstrap's
  `data-bs-toggle="tab"` keeps working unchanged.
- Every tab button starts with a Bootstrap icon
  (`<i class="bi bi-<icon> me-1"></i>`) followed by the label.
  Never an icon-less tab.
- The active state has no fill and no radius. Active colour, weight, and
  underline are owned by `.ob-tabs`; never override `.nav-link.active` in an
  area stylesheet.
- Two densities are allowed:
  - Standard: `.ob-tabs` (`0.8125rem`, `0.5rem 0`, `1.5rem` gap) for pages,
    cards, settings, and modals.
  - Compact: `.ob-tabs.ob-tabs--compact` (`0.75rem`, `0.35rem 0`, `1rem` gap)
    for narrow right-hand panels and generated panel tabs.
- Layout helpers (`nav-fill`, `flex-shrink-0`, `mb-0`) may accompany
  `ob-tabs`. The catalogued combinations are:
  - `nav nav-tabs ob-tabs mb-0` — independent Domain / Settings surfaces.
  - `nav nav-tabs ob-tabs` — Import / Home Registry tabs.
  - `nav nav-tabs ob-tabs nav-fill` — Domain → General / Ontology Generate /
    Ontology DQ / Business Rules / Axioms.
  - `nav nav-tabs ob-tabs nav-fill flex-shrink-0` —
    DT-DataQuality / DT-Inference (the `flex-shrink-0` keeps the
    tab strip a fixed-size header inside their flex card body).
  - `nav nav-tabs ob-tabs ob-tabs--compact` — Ontology Studio and Mapping
    Studio generated forms.
  - `nav nav-tabs ob-tabs ob-tabs--compact sg-panel-tabs` — KG Explorer.
- Do not add `.small` or per-area font/padding declarations. Select the
  standard or compact density instead.
- Do not re-apply spacing or background utilities (`px-3`, `pt-2`, `pt-3`,
  `bg-white`) on the tab `<ul>`.
- Do **not** add `style="gap: 2px;"` (or any inline `style`)
  on the tab `<ul>` — the gap of 0 is set by `ob-tabs` and is
  what makes the underline read as one continuous baseline.
- The tab content can sit inside a Bootstrap `.card` or stand alone. For
  independent surfaces, never visually fuse the rail to a following card by
  removing its top border or top radii.
- **Full-height pattern (preferred for tabbed pages).** Tabs +
  body should fill the page from below the section header to the
  shared sidebar/shell bottom (the 0.5rem viewport gutter),
  exactly like the single card on Ontology > General. Two
  ways to achieve this:
  1. Place the `<ul ob-tabs>` and the body card as **direct
     children of `.content-section`**. The existing
     `sidebar-layout.css` rule
     `.sidebar-section > .content-section > .card { flex: 1 }`
     stretches the body card to fill (Domain / Settings /
     Ontology Import / DT Inference work this way).
  2. Wrap the strip and the body in a `<div class="ob-tabs-wrap">`
     when you need the same fill behaviour deeper in the markup
     (e.g. inside another flex column). `ob-tabs-wrap` is a
     flex-column container that pins the strip via
     `flex-shrink: 0` and gives `flex: 1 + overflow-y: auto` to
     its child `.tab-content` / `.ob-tab-content` / `.card`.
     When `ob-tabs-wrap` is itself a direct child of
     `.content-section`, it grows to fill the section.
- `.ob-tab-content` is an optional independent bordered surface with complete
  card rounding. New page tabs should default to the card-integrated pattern
  and plain `.tab-content` inside their parent card.

**Anti-patterns**

- ❌ Bare `<ul class="nav nav-tabs">` without `ob-tabs`.
- ❌ Pill tabs (`nav-pills`) anywhere in the app.
- ❌ Per-area redeclaration of `.nav-link` colour, padding,
  border-bottom, or `.nav-link.active` styling. The whole point
  of `ob-tabs` is one shared treatment.
- ❌ Active fills, rounded selected items, or an outer rounded/filled tab
  strip.
- ❌ Re-applying `px-3 pt-2` / `px-3 pt-3` / `bg-white` on the tab `<ul>`.
- ❌ Adding Bootstrap's `.small` (or any `font-size` override) on
  the tab `<ul>` or `.nav-link`; use `ob-tabs--compact`.
- ❌ Removing the top border/radius from a following card to fuse it with the
  tab rail.
- ❌ A standalone `.ob-tab-content` directly below the rail when both belong to
  one logical card workspace. Use the card-integrated page-tabs skeleton.
- ❌ A short, free-floating tabs-card that doesn't fill the page
  height. Use the *Full-height pattern* above (direct children of
  `.content-section`, or `.ob-tabs-wrap`) so the tabs reach the
  shared sidebar/shell bottom (the 0.5rem viewport gutter) like
  the Ontology > General card.
- ❌ Inline `style="gap:…"` or `style="font-size:…"` on the tab
  `<ul>`.
- ❌ New hand-rolled tab engines. Use Bootstrap's `data-bs-toggle="tab"`. The
  existing Ontology form adapter may remain, but it must emit the shared
  compact classes and preserve `role`, `aria-selected`, and one active pane.
- ❌ Tabs without icons or without `aria-selected` /
  `aria-controls`.
- ❌ Adding tab styles to `main.css`'s `.nav-tabs` block —
  that's the legacy fallback. New work goes through
  `.ob-tabs` in `components.css`.

### Run Lists (`.ob-runs-table`) — Databricks Jobs → Runs style

Graph → Build → Runs, Intelligence → Analytics → Runs and Settings →
Automation → Runs share one flat list modelled on the Databricks Jobs → Runs
page. Rows come only from `global/js/runs-render.js` (`RunsRender.*RunCells`);
styling lives only in `components.css`.

- Toolbar (`.ob-runs-toolbar`): run count (`.ob-runs-count`, e.g. `12 runs`)
  on the left, Refresh on the right. Settings keeps its section-header Refresh
  and Context filter.
- Table: `table table-hover align-middle ob-runs-table`, plain `<thead>` (no
  `table-light`, no inline `font-size` or column widths). Header 12px
  semibold `--db-text-secondary` on a transparent row with a 1px
  `--db-border` underline; cells 13px with `--db-border-light` hairlines;
  hover `--db-hover`.
- Columns use sentence case and **Start time** is always first, rendered as a
  `--db-link` link (`.ob-run-link`) that opens the run details. Build runs
  also show **Run ID** and **Duration**. The last column is a ghost
  `.ob-run-details-btn` (visually hidden "Details" header).
- Status is icon + word (`RunsRender.runStatus` → `.ob-run-status.is-*`):
  Succeeded (success), Failed (danger), Running (info), Canceled / Pending
  (muted). Version is plain `vN` text (`.ob-run-version`). Filled badges
  (`*StatusBadge`, `versionBadge`) stay for the run-details modals only.
- ❌ Filled status pills, grey header bands, or outline-primary eye buttons in
  run-list rows.

### Work-List Template (`.ob-filter-bar` + `.ob-runs-table`) — Databricks Query History style

This is the **app-wide template for every flat work list**: a filter bar above
one compact, hoverable table. Styles live in `global/css/components.css`;
the free-text filter helper is `global/js/list-filter.js` (`ObListFilter.bind`).
Used by Context → My Tasks, Graph → Build → Runs, Intelligence → Analytics →
Runs, and the Home → My tasks tile (table only). Settings → Runs lists keep
the same table but have no filter bar yet.

- Filter bar (`.ob-filter-bar`, flex row with `gap: 0.5rem`):
  - search: `.ob-filter-search` wrapping a leading `bi-search` icon and an
    `input[type=search].form-control-sm`; filtered by `ObListFilter.bind`
    on the rows' visible text.
  - filter dropdowns (My Tasks only): Bootstrap `.dropdown` with a
    `.ob-filter-btn` toggle that shows `Label: Value` and gains `.is-active`.
  - row count: `.ob-runs-count` (`N runs`, or `N of M runs` while filtering).
  - a trailing action such as Refresh gets `ms-auto` to sit on the right.
- Table: `table table-hover align-middle ob-runs-table`, plain `<thead>`.
  Rows are compact: cell padding `0.3rem 0.75rem`, row buttons `btn-sm` with
  tighter vertical padding (see `.ob-runs-table .btn-sm`).
- Re-apply the filter (`ObListFilter.bind(...).apply()`) after every render
  so the count stays accurate.
- **Status** is icon + word via `.ob-run-status.is-*`; no filled badges.
  Row actions are `btn-sm btn-outline-secondary`.
- Task-specific bits only (e.g. `.ob-tasks-title`, `.ob-tasks-meta`) live in
  `domain/css/domain-collaboration.css`.
- ❌ Per-list copies of the filter bar or table styles, separate tables with
  uppercase captions, or filled status badges.

### Split-Panel Canvas Layout (canvas + resizable right panel)

Every screen that pairs a canvas or a browsable list with a contextual
right-hand panel uses one layout, defined by the Knowledge Graph Explorer
(`query/css/query-sigmagraph.css`) and shared by Ontology Studio /
Entities / Relationships (`ontology/css/ontology-shared-panels.css`).

The contract:

- **Flex row with a `0.5rem` gutter.** Content is `flex: 1` with a
  `min-width` so it can't be crushed; the panel is `flex-shrink: 0`.
- **Two framed panes, one frameless shell.** The wrapping `.card` carries
  `ob-split-shell` (transparent, no shadow); each pane carries the framing
  — `ob-split-pane` for the content side, matched by `.shared-detail-panel`
  on the right. Both are white with `1px solid var(--db-border)` and
  `var(--db-radius-card)`. Never frame the shell *and* the panes.
- **The panel is permanent, not a drawer.** `min-width: 200px`,
  `max-width: 50%`; the default width is `320px` for a read-only details
  panel (Explorer) and `420px` where the panel hosts edit forms (Ontology),
  which gives the compact tabs a comfortable default. The shared rail
  remains on one line and scrolls horizontally when the panel is narrower.
  It is created *before* the view renders — canvases read their width once
  (D3 / Sigma size from `container.clientWidth`), so a panel that appears
  later leaves the graph centred on the wrong axis.
- **Empty state instead of collapsing.** With nothing selected the panel
  title is **Select Item** with no leading icon (same copy as Mapping Studio) and the body
  holds a centred placeholder (`bi-cursor` icon at `3rem`,
  `--db-text-muted`, "Click on an entity or relationship to view
  details"). Clicking the empty canvas returns to that placeholder — and
  flushes any pending edit first, since it is the only way out.
  A selected entity or relationship title has no pencil icon.
  Tabs inside the panel use `.ob-tabs.ob-tabs--compact`; area styles may retain
  only layout rules such as `flex-shrink`. Typography, spacing, overflow,
  focus, hover, and active states remain owned by the shared component.
- **No close button.** The panel can't be dismissed, so a cross would be a
  dead control.
- **Drag handle**: a `1px` track holding a `0.5px × 40px` bar,
  overlapping `0.25rem` of the `0.5rem` gutter on each side,
  `--db-border` at rest and `--db-primary` on hover or while dragging. The
  drag clamp must span the default width (Ontology: `200px`–`900px`).
- **Host the panel on the wrapper, not on the canvas.** It is a *sibling*
  of the content pane; making the canvas container the flex parent means it
  can no longer be framed as a pane.
- **Mapping Studio uses the same split cards as Ontology**, but in
  `.ob-split-shell--column`: content pane on top and an always-visible bottom
  detail panel with the shared `detail-panel-resize-handle`.

❌ Don't reintroduce a `width: 0` + `.panel-open` drawer, a per-area handle
with its own dimensions or `--bs-primary` blue, or a `gap: 0` seam between
canvas and panel.

### Component Naming

| Prefix | Owner | Use |
|--------|-------|-----|
| `ob-*` | OntoBricks shared components | Cross-page reusable widgets (`ob-kpi-tile`, `ob-breadcrumb`, `ob-shortcut-card`, `ob-loading-spinner`, `ob-tabs`, `ob-tab-content`) |
| `ovz-*` | OntoViz canvas | Entities, relationships, toolbar, context menu inside the OntoViz library |
| Bootstrap classes | Bootstrap 5.3.2 | Base utilities and layout primitives |
| Area-specific | Per-area CSS files | Local concerns; prefer to first reuse an `ob-*` component |

When introducing a new shared component:

1. Pick the `ob-*` prefix.
2. Place styles in `global/css/components.css` (or a new file under
   `global/css/` if the component is large enough to deserve its own file
   and load it in `base.html`).
3. Document the component contract in a short comment block at the top of
   its CSS section (selector skeleton + variants), in the style of
   `.ob-kpi-tile` in `components.css`.

### Reusable Components Catalog

These already exist; reuse before reinventing.

- **KPI tile** — `.ob-kpi-tile` with optional state variants
  (`.tile-success`, `.tile-danger`, `.tile-warning`, `.tile-muted`) and
  bottom-accent variants (`.tile-accent-*`). Sub-elements:
  `.ob-kpi-tile-icon`, `.ob-kpi-tile-value`, `.ob-kpi-tile-label`.
- **Loading spinner** — one brand spinner app-wide. Inline: plain Bootstrap
  `.spinner-border` (auto-repainted to the brand spinner by `main.css`).
  Large/centered: `.ob-loading-spinner` with `.ob-spinner-svg` and optional
  `.ob-spinner-label` (SVG markup is in `base.html` — do not redraw the
  rings/diamond — include the partial or copy verbatim). See
  *Loading Spinners* for the full contract.
- **Page-level loading overlay** — `.domain-loading-overlay` already wired
  in `base.html` as `#domainLoadingOverlay`. Toggle via `.d-none`.
- **Save Domain button** — `#menuSaveDomain` (`.ob-subnav-save-btn`) in the
  L2 subnav. Enabled/disabled by `updateVersionStatus()` in
  `domain-actions.js`. Do not add a second Save button on a page.
- **New Domain dialog** — `showNewDomainDialog()` (from `utils.js`). Returns
  `{ name, description, llm_endpoint }` or `null`.
- **Tabs** — `.nav.nav-tabs.ob-tabs` (transparent underline rail).
  One shared visual treatment and two densities are defined in
  `components.css`; see *Tabs (`.ob-tabs`) — transparent underline rail*
  above. `.ob-tab-content` is the optional independent bordered surface.
- **Cards** — Bootstrap `.card` / `.card-header`; styling already neutered
  to project tokens (`2px` radius, one-pixel border, no resting shadow).
- **Forms** — Bootstrap `.form-control` / `.form-select` / `.form-label`;
  one-pixel warm borders, white/warm surfaces, `2px` radius, themed focus ring.
- **Alerts** — Bootstrap `.alert` with `.alert-success` / `.alert-danger`
  using project status background tokens.
- **Wizard overlay** — `.wizard-form-overlay` for absolute-positioned
  blocking spinners over a form during async tasks.
- **Keyboard shortcut overlay** — `.ob-shortcut-overlay` + `.ob-shortcut-card`.

### Loading Spinners

There is **one** loading-spinner look across the whole app: the **OntoBricks
brand spinner** (the rotating node-ring + diamond). Never introduce a second
spinner style.

**How it works.** A global override in `global/css/main.css` repaints every
Bootstrap `.spinner-border` / `.spinner-grow` into the brand spinner (it drops
the border + Bootstrap rotation and paints
`background: url(/static/global/img/loading-spinner.svg)`; the SVG self-animates
via SMIL). So **the standard way to show an inline spinner is plain Bootstrap
markup** — it is automatically branded, at every call site, present and future:

```html
<div class="spinner-border" role="status" aria-hidden="true"></div>
<!-- small variant -->
<div class="spinner-border spinner-border-sm" role="status" aria-hidden="true"></div>
```

Size is controlled by Bootstrap's own `width/height` (and the `-sm` modifier) —
do not set a custom size that fights the override.

**Pick the right tool:**

| Need | Use |
|------|-----|
| Inline spinner in a button, cell, or while a section loads | Bootstrap `.spinner-border` (auto-branded). Add a label in a sibling `<span>` if needed. |
| Large, centered spinner with a caption (panel/empty-state) | `.ob-loading-spinner` + `.ob-spinner-svg` + optional `.ob-spinner-label`. SVG markup lives in `base.html` — include the partial or copy it **verbatim**; never redraw the rings/diamond. |
| Full-page domain load | The existing `#domainLoadingOverlay` / `.domain-loading-overlay` in `base.html` (toggle `.d-none`). |

`.spin-animation` (a rotating **status** icon for running tasks) is **not** a
loading spinner — leave it as is.

**Don'ts**

- ❌ A new spinner style — custom `@keyframes` ring, a third-party/SVG spinner
  library, an emoji/`bi-hourglass` "loading" glyph, or an `<img>` of a
  different spinner. Use the Bootstrap spinner (auto-branded) or
  `.ob-loading-spinner`.
- ❌ Overriding `.spinner-border` / `.spinner-grow` to re-add a border, change
  the animation, or repaint the background — that defeats the single brand
  spinner. The override lives once in `main.css`.
- ❌ Redrawing the brand spinner SVG (`loading-spinner.svg` / the
  `.ob-spinner-svg` rings + diamond) in page code.
- ❌ Wrapping spinners and loading text in a separate background container
  (e.g. Bootstrap `.alert-secondary`, rounded grey boxes, or bordered wells).
  Spinners and loading labels must sit seamlessly on the parent surface
  (transparent background, muted/standard text). Never use a separate
  background (in rounded boxes) for spinners or loading states anywhere in
  the app.

### Popups, Modals, Confirmations & Notifications

**Two non-negotiable rules:**

1. **Native browser popups are forbidden.** Never call `alert()`,
   `confirm()`, or `prompt()` from any frontend code. They bypass our
   design system, block the main thread, look like a 2003 Windows error,
   and cannot be tested from JS.
2. **All user-facing messages route through the navbar Activity
   Center.** There is one message bus for the whole app — the inboxes
   dropdown in the top-right of the navbar (`#activityCenterDropdown`),
   backed by the `NotificationCenter` singleton in
   `global/js/utils.js` (and the Task Tracker list in the same panel).
   Every success / error / warning / info that a user should see goes
   through `showNotification(...)`. No per-page toasts. No inline
   `<div class="alert">` blocks bolted on as a status line. No
   console-only errors for things the user needs to know.

#### Single message bus — the navbar Activity Center

The Activity Center is provided by `base.html` and runs on every
page:

- Toggle: `#activityCenterToggle` (icon `bi-inboxes`).
- Dropdown: `#activityCenterDropdown` with `#taskTrackerList` (running
  jobs) above `#notifCenterList` (messages).
- Combined unread/active badge: `#activityCenterBadge` (auto-managed;
  shows `99+` past 99).
- Buffer: in-memory ring buffer capped by
  `<html data-max-notifications>` (default `10`).
- Each notification shows icon + message + relative time
  (`just now`, `5s ago`, `12m ago`, `3h ago`, then absolute date).
- Opening the dropdown marks all notifications read; refresh reloads
  tasks; trash clears notifications.

**The only API you should call is `showNotification(message, type,
duration?)`.** It pushes to the centre **and** flashes a transient
toast for `success` / `warning` / `error` so urgent messages are
immediately visible without forcing the user to open the panel.

#### What goes through the Notification Center

| Source of the message | Route via |
|------------------------|-----------|
| API call result (success / failure) | `showNotification(...)` in the page's `.then` / `.catch` |
| Form validation error before submit | `showNotification(msg, 'warning')` (then keep the form open) |
| Background task progress / completion | Already wired via the Task Tracker (`task-tracker.js`) — listen, don't reinvent |
| Confirmation of a destructive action | `await showDeleteConfirm(name, type)` — its outcome should still produce a `showNotification` when done |
| Question requiring user choice | `await showConfirmDialog({...})` |
| Free-text input | A purpose-built Bootstrap modal with a `<form>` — never `prompt()` |
| Long-form details (preview, help) | A Bootstrap modal (`DocumentPreview.open(...)` or area-specific) |

#### What to use instead

| You want to… | Use | Where it's defined |
|--------------|-----|---------------------|
| Tell the user something happened (info / success / warning / error) | `showNotification(message, type, duration?)` | `global/js/utils.js` |
| Ask Yes/No | `await showConfirmDialog({ title, message, confirmText, cancelText, confirmClass, icon })` | `global/js/utils.js` |
| Confirm a delete | `await showDeleteConfirm(itemName, itemType?)` (red themed) | `global/js/utils.js` |
| Preview a file | `DocumentPreview.open(filename)` | `global/js/utils.js` |
| Get a string from the user | A purpose-built Bootstrap modal with a `<form>` and an explicit Cancel/Save footer — never `prompt()`. |
| Modal with custom content | A Bootstrap 5 modal in a Jinja partial, opened via `bootstrap.Modal.getOrCreateInstance(modalEl).show()` |

`showNotification` already pushes the message into the navbar
Notification Center bell **and** displays a transient toast for
`error` / `warning` / `success`. Use it as the single entry point for
all user-facing feedback. Toast styling lives in `pages.css`
(`.error-toast-popup`, `.warning-toast-popup`, `.success-toast-popup`,
`#errorToastContainer`) — do not reimplement.

#### Type → toast policy

| `type` | Notification Center | Floating toast | Default duration |
|--------|---------------------|----------------|------------------|
| `info` | yes | no | n/a |
| `success` | yes | yes | 3000 ms |
| `warning` | yes | yes | 4000 ms |
| `error` | yes | yes | 6000 ms |

Messages ending in `...` are treated as transient progress messages and
are deliberately **not** added to the Notification Center — only shown
as toasts. Use this for "Saving...", "Loading...", etc.

#### Bootstrap modal pattern (for purpose-built dialogs)

Every modal that's not produced by a helper above must:

1. Live in a partial under `templates/partials/<area>/_<area>_<feature>_modal.html`
   (or a shared one under `templates/partials/layout/` if cross-area),
   and be `{% include %}`'d by the page template.
2. Use the standard Bootstrap structure:
   ```html
   <div class="modal fade" id="<purpose>Modal" tabindex="-1">
       <div class="modal-dialog modal-dialog-centered">
           <div class="modal-content">
               <div class="modal-header">
                   <h5 class="modal-title">
                       <i class="bi bi-<icon> me-2"></i>Title
                   </h5>
                   <button type="button" class="btn-close" data-bs-dismiss="modal"></button>
               </div>
               <div class="modal-body">...</div>
               <div class="modal-footer">
                   <button type="button" class="btn btn-secondary" data-bs-dismiss="modal">Cancel</button>
                   <button type="button" class="btn btn-primary" id="<purpose>SaveBtn">Save</button>
               </div>
           </div>
       </div>
   </div>
   ```
3. Be opened from JS via:
   ```js
   const modalEl = document.getElementById('<purpose>Modal');
   const modal = bootstrap.Modal.getOrCreateInstance(modalEl);
   modal.show();
   ```
   Use `getOrCreateInstance` (idempotent), never `new bootstrap.Modal(...)`
   each time.
4. Always include the `data-bs-dismiss="modal"` button-close pattern so
   Esc / backdrop click / Cancel all behave consistently.
5. Never use inline `style="..."` on the modal — if a custom width is
   needed, use Bootstrap's `modal-lg` / `modal-xl` modifier or add a
   class in the matching CSS file.

#### Reusing existing modals before building a new one

Before adding a new modal, check whether one of these can be reused:

- `partials/layout/uc_file_browser_modal.html` — Unity Catalog file
  picker (`UCFileDialog.open({ mode: 'load' | 'save', ... })`).
- `partials/layout/help_modal.html` — Help Center.
- `DocumentPreview.open(filename)` — generic document viewer
  (PDF / image / text).
- `showConfirmDialog(...)` / `showDeleteConfirm(...)` — yes/no prompts.

If the use case fits one of these (even loosely), extend it rather than
duplicating modal scaffolding.

### Static Asset Folder Layout

```
src/front/static/
├── global/                <- Cross-page assets
│   ├── css/               <- main.css, components.css, pages.css,
│   │                         sidebar-layout.css, permissions.css, …
│   ├── js/                <- navbar.js, sidebar-nav.js, breadcrumb.js,
│   │                         permissions.js, utils.js, task-tracker.js, …
│   ├── img/               <- logos, favicons, spinners (SVG)
│   └── ontoviz/           <- The OntoViz library (CSS + JS together)
│       ├── css/
│       └── ontoviz.js
├── <area>/                <- One folder per area
│   ├── css/               <- <area>-<feature>.css (e.g. query-cohorts.css)
│   └── js/                <- <area>-<feature>.js
└── config/
```

**Area folders mirror partial folders.** A new file at
`templates/partials/<area>/_<feature>.html` should pair with assets at
`static/<area>/{css,js}/<area>-<feature>.{css,js}`.

### Asset Wiring (in `base.html` / page templates)

- Always cache-bust with `?v={{ asset_version }}`. The bare `?v=2` literal
  is reserved for the favicon.
- Load order in `base.html` is fixed; do not reorder:
  1. Bootstrap CSS (CDN)
  2. Bootstrap Icons (CDN)
  3. `main.css` → `components.css` → `pages.css` → `sidebar-layout.css`
     → `emoji-picker.css` → `help-modal.css`
  4. `permissions.css` **must load LAST** so its `!important` role/version
     gates win over per-component rules.
- `permissions.js` **must be the first deferred script** so
  `window.OB.permissions` is defined before navbar/version-check read it.
- Per-page CSS/JS goes in `{% block extra_css %}` / `{% block extra_js %}`,
  also cache-busted.

### Read-Only & Role Gating

- Role/version gating is centralised in `global/css/permissions.css` and
  driven by body classes set by `permissions.js`:
  `body.read-only-version`, `body.role-viewer`.
- Inputs and action affordances are hidden / disabled via `body:is(...)`
  selectors with `!important`. Do not duplicate this logic per page.
- Server side, `data-app-role`, `data-domain-role` and `data-app-mode` are
  set on `<body>` in `base.html` — read them from JS rather than calling
  the API again.

### Accessibility Baseline

- Every interactive icon-only control has a `title=` (and ideally
  `aria-label=`). See navbar bell / hourglass / help icons.
- Status semantics must include icon + label (and optional tint); color alone
  is not an acceptable signal.
- Decorative SVGs (chevrons, spinner glyphs) have `aria-hidden="true"`.
- Modals use Bootstrap's modal markup so focus trapping and ESC are free.
- Maintain Bootstrap's contrast: do not lighten text below
  `--db-text-muted` for body copy.

### What NOT to Do

- ❌ `alert()`, `confirm()`, `prompt()` — use `showNotification`,
  `showConfirmDialog`, `showDeleteConfirm`, or a Bootstrap modal.
- ❌ A per-page toast container, alert banner, or status `<div>` for
  user feedback. Use the navbar Notification Center.
- ❌ `console.error(...)` as the only signal that something failed —
  mirror it with a `showNotification(msg, 'error')`.
- ❌ `new bootstrap.Modal(el)` on each open — use
  `bootstrap.Modal.getOrCreateInstance(el)`.
- ❌ Per-page reimplementation of the navbar, breadcrumb, KPI tile,
  loading spinner, modal scaffolding, sidebar layout, notification
  centre, task tracker, help centre, or UC file browser.
- ❌ A page template that does not `{% extends "base.html" %}`.
- ❌ Top-level page markup that is neither `.sidebar-layout` nor a
  page-header + standalone block — no third pattern.
- ❌ Action buttons placed anywhere other than the top-right of the
  section's `.section-header`. No floating FABs, no bottom action
  bars, no buttons inside `.card-header`.
- ❌ Hand-coded sidebar `<a class="nav-link">` items — use
  `_sidebar_nav.html` + `menu_config.json`.
- ❌ `addEventListener('click', ...)` on sidebar nav links — use the
  `sidebarSectionChanged` event or `onBeforeSectionChange` hook.
- ❌ Setting `style.display` on `.sidebar-section` directly — toggle
  the `.active` class via `SidebarNav.switchTo(...)`.
- ❌ Writing `--ob-chrome-height` from page code — `breadcrumb.js` /
  `updateDomainMenuVisibility` own it.
- ❌ Pane-level `100vh` on sidebar pages, extra content `padding-bottom`
  that breaks menu/pane bottom alignment, or restoring
  `.sidebar-nav { margin: 0.5rem; height: calc(100% - 1rem); }`.
- ❌ Toggling `#obSubnav` from page code.
- ❌ Restoring L2 workspace dropdown tabs beside the Context crumb — use
  `#obAppSwitcher` (Graph + Intelligence items keep `nav-requires-graph`).
- ❌ A Save Domain button anywhere outside `#obSubnav`.
- ❌ Calling `alert()` / `prompt()` for new-domain input — use
  `showNewDomainDialog()` from `utils.js`.
- ❌ Bare `<ul class="nav nav-tabs">` (no `ob-tabs`), pill tabs, active
  fills, rounded selected tabs, or per-area redeclaration of `.nav-link` /
  `.nav-link.active` styles. All tab nav uses the transparent underline rail
  from `components.css` (see *Tabs (`.ob-tabs`) — transparent underline
  rail*).
- ❌ A second spinner style. Use the single brand spinner — inline via
  Bootstrap `.spinner-border` (auto-branded) or `.ob-loading-spinner` for
  large/centered cases. No custom keyframes, spinner libraries, emoji/
  `bi-hourglass` "loading" glyphs, or `.spinner-border` overrides (see
  *Loading Spinners*).
- ❌ Inline `<style>` or `style="…"` in templates. Move to
  `static/<area>/css/`.
- ❌ Inline `<script>` blocks beyond a single tiny bootstrap call. Move
  to `static/<area>/js/`.
- ❌ Hard-coded hex/rgb colours when a token exists.
- ❌ Hard-coding cold-grey values in app-shell UI when a warm `--db-*` token
  exists for the same role (surface, border, hover, muted text, or separator).
- ❌ Importing webfonts, FontAwesome, Material Icons, or any
  non-Bootstrap icon font.
- ❌ Keeping all buttons visually identical/neutral when a section needs a
  rightmost filled primary action (`.btn-primary`).
- ❌ Using semantic red as a generic primary accent (reserve it for destructive
  outline semantics or explicit danger states).
- ❌ Multiple competing accent hues for app-shell emphasis; use the Clarity
  indigo token family.
- ❌ Resting card shadows or two-pixel resting borders on cards/forms/tables.
- ❌ Leaving configuration or readable forms unbounded/sparse on wide canvases;
  use a bounded readable width or the shared sidebar/content layout patterns.
- ❌ Status color as the only signal (missing icon + label text).
- ❌ Mixing `--db-*` and `--ovz-*` tokens in the same selector.
- ❌ Loading `permissions.css` anywhere other than last, or
  `permissions.js` anywhere other than first-defer.
- ❌ Sidebar nav items hand-coded in templates instead of via
  `menu_config.json` + `_sidebar_nav.html`.
- ❌ Inline modal markup created from JS string templates for ad-hoc
  dialogs — put the markup in a partial and `{% include %}` it.
